ors  mike pompeo and gina haspel and also an  epilogue that  explains what the president knew and  when he knew it and what the  intelligence community told him  and among other things there was an  almost total breakdown of the  presidential briefing process in january  where he was having literally one verbal  briefing  a week now if you talk to any previous  president they'll tell you that's that's  almost a dereliction of duty  chris the the president has said that  that the cia told him the virus was not  a big deal  that's not what you heard well you know  you know you know  it was interesting because he said on  fox news  in effect he threw his intelligence  briefer beth center under the bus  he said that she downplayed the virus  now i think that's a case where  a competent c.i.a director  would either come to that briefer's  defense  or at least brief reporters on whether  or not  she did in fact warn him about the  coronavirus  that statement by trump was wrong on so  many levels  and it points to uh one of the  disappointments about the current  director gina haspel who  came in with really high hopes uh she is  a fascinating character  a mystery woman who flies under the  radar is given no interviews but i have  some stories about her when she was at  the black site the infamous cia black  site in thailand  uh where she struck up an interesting  rapport with one of the key al qaeda  terrorists  uh she's a fascinating story chris tony  decopol here since you mentioned the  president uh threw  an analyst under the bus as you put it  it brings to mind the other clashes he's  had in public with the intelligence  community  what impact do those clashes have on the  work of the cia  well the cia is pretty good at keeping  its head down  and ignoring who the president may be at  any given time in the white house but  there's no question about it i mean this  has been a broken marriage from day one  you remember when  the president went to langley and stood  in front of the most hallowed ground  at cia headquarters the memorial wall  and he bragged about the size of his  inauguration  and barely acknowledged the fallen cia  officers  it's been pretty rough ever since  but it's not the first time that a  president has thought that the cia was a  deep state  full of liberal enemies who were out to  bring him down that's what richard nixon  thought about  dick helms who was an iconic director  back in the 60s  and he's also a fascinating story in the  book yeah and as  chris whipple thank you as we pointed  out that you spoke to every living  director  except uh mike pompeo and gina haspel  who  were ci directors under president trump  thanks so much for being with us the spy  masters  goes on sale tomorrow and you can hear  chris whipple talk more about the  spymasters with cbs news chief justice  and homeland security correspondent jeff  begues on today's  cbs this morning podcast
 

